\section{State Definition}
\label{sec:state}

The nominal quaternion is stored separately from the additive navigation and
bias states.  For $q\in\Sph^3$,
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:nominal-state-vector}
\vct{x}_{\mathrm{nom}} =
\begin{bmatrix}
q &
\vct{b}_g^{\top} &
\vct{v}^{\top} &
\vct{p}^{\top} &
\vct{S}^{\top} &
\vct{a}_w^{\top} &
\vct{b}_a^{\top}
\end{bmatrix}^{\!\top}.
\end{equation}
The quaternion is propagated and corrected on $\Sph^3$, not by vector
addition.

The covariance is defined for the local error state
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:state-vector}
\delta\vct{x} =
\begin{bmatrix}
\delta\vct{\theta}^{\top} &
\delta\vct{b}_g^{\top} &
\delta\vct{v}^{\top} &
\delta\vct{p}^{\top} &
\delta\vct{S}^{\top} &
\delta\vct{a}_w^{\top} &
\delta\vct{b}_a^{\top}
\end{bmatrix}^{\!\top},
\end{equation}
where
\begin{itemize}
  \item $\delta\vct{\theta}\in\mathbb{R}^3$ is the small
        left-multiplicative attitude error used by the MEKF
        \cite{Shuster1993_AttitudeRepresentations,MarkleyCrassidis2014_Attitude};
  \item $\vct{b}_g\in\mathbb{R}^3$ is gyroscope bias, modeled as a random walk;
  \item $\vct{v}\in\mathbb{R}^3$ is velocity in the world NED frame;
  \item $\vct{p}\in\mathbb{R}^3$ is position or displacement in the world frame;
  \item $\vct{S}\in\mathbb{R}^3$ is the displacement integral
        $\vct{S}(t)=\int_0^t\vct{p}(s)\,ds$ used by the drift-control
        pseudo-measurement;
  \item $\vct{a}_w\in\mathbb{R}^3$ is latent world-frame acceleration with an
        OU prior;
  \item $\vct{b}_a\in\mathbb{R}^3$ is residual accelerometer bias about the
        deterministic temperature-calibration curve, modeled as a slow
        first-order Gauss--Markov/OU process.  The total bias is
        $\vct b_a^{\mathrm{tot}}(T)=\vct k_a(T-T_{\mathrm{ref}})+\vct b_a$.
\end{itemize}

With both bias states enabled, $N_X=21$ and
$\mat{P}=\E[\delta\vct{x}\,\delta\vct{x}^{\top}]$.  The residual
accelerometer-bias correlation time is $\tau_b$.

Two acceleration covariances must be distinguished.
$\matg{\Sigma}_{aw}^{\mathrm{stat}}$ is the prescribed stationary covariance
of the OU prior and determines $\matg{Q}_d$, whereas $\mat{P}_{a_wa_w}$ is the
posterior uncertainty of the estimated acceleration state.  Changing
$\matg{\Sigma}_{aw}^{\mathrm{stat}}$ does not overwrite
$\mat{P}_{a_wa_w}$.  Hard initialization may reset this posterior block and its
cross-covariances; default online maintenance only adds the positive-semidefinite
covariance required to reach the current stationary floor.
